{% doc %}
  Renders a "X% claimed" progress bar for a daily deal, from real inventory.

  There are two honest ways to count what has been claimed, and which one
  applies depends on who is holding the stock.

  DROPSHIP / COLLECTIVE (the normal case here). The supplier holds the
  inventory and it is enormous relative to the deal — a Collective product on
  this store carries 14,357 units. Inventory does not start at our allocation,
  so we snapshot it when the deal opens and measure the delta:

      claimed = starting_inventory - inventory on hand now
      percent = claimed / units_allocated

  That delta is real units sold while the deal was running.

  SELF-STOCKED. When no snapshot is recorded we assume the allocation IS the
  stock, which is true if you hold it yourself:

      claimed = units_allocated - inventory on hand now

  Renders NOTHING when it cannot be truthful:
    * no allocation set
    * the product does not track inventory in Shopify

  A caveat worth knowing: the supplier can restock mid-deal, which would make
  the delta understate what sold. It cannot overstate it, so the bar errs
  toward showing less urgency than is real — the safe direction.

  @param {object} product - The deal product.
  @param {number} units_allocated - Units committed to this deal. The divisor.
  @param {number} [starting_inventory] - Inventory on hand when the deal opened.
  @param {number} [floor_percent] - Never display below this. Off by default.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign allocated = units_allocated | plus: 0
  assign snapshot = starting_inventory | plus: 0
  assign tracked = false
  assign remaining = 0

  comment
    Sum across variants. A variant with inventory_management unset is untracked,
    and Shopify reports inventory_quantity 0 for those — counting it would read
    as "sold out" and wrongly push the bar to 100%.
  endcomment
  for variant in product.variants
    if variant.inventory_management == 'shopify'
      assign tracked = true
      if variant.inventory_quantity > 0
        assign remaining = remaining | plus: variant.inventory_quantity
      endif
    endif
  endfor

  if snapshot > 0
    assign claimed = snapshot | minus: remaining
  else
    assign claimed = allocated | minus: remaining
  endif

  comment
    Negative means stock went UP since the snapshot (a supplier restock), or the
    allocation is stale. Report nothing sold rather than a negative bar.
  endcomment
  if claimed < 0
    assign claimed = 0
  endif

  assign percent = 0
  if allocated > 0
    assign percent = claimed | times: 100 | divided_by: allocated
    if percent > 100
      assign percent = 100
    endif
  endif

  comment
    Units left in OUR allocation — not the supplier's shelf. On a dropship deal
    "Only 14,357 left" is absurd; "Only 12 left" is the number that is both
    true and meaningful, because the allocation is what we will honour.
  endcomment
  assign units_left = allocated | minus: claimed
  if units_left < 0
    assign units_left = 0
  endif

  assign display_percent = percent
  if floor_percent and display_percent < floor_percent
    assign display_percent = floor_percent
  endif

  assign show = false
  if tracked and allocated > 0
    assign show = true
  endif
-%}

{{ "aj-deal.css" | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{%- if show -%}
  <div class="aj-claimed">
    <div
      class="aj-claimed__track"
      role="progressbar"
      aria-valuenow="{{ display_percent }}"
      aria-valuemin="0"
      aria-valuemax="100"
      aria-label="{{ display_percent }}% of this deal claimed"
    >
      <div class="aj-claimed__fill" style="width: {{ display_percent }}%;"></div>
    </div>
    <p class="aj-claimed__text">
      <strong>{{ display_percent }}% claimed</strong>
      {%- if units_left > 0 and units_left <= 25 -%}
        <span class="aj-claimed__remaining">Only {{ units_left }} left</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </p>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
